h again you know I'll Point  towards the  um sources that you know or the  references that is  oh about 15 pages roughly uh in the back  you know so you can follow up your own  work I also think that  um the summaries that hernia carniadis  is providing of these sources is really  quite good too it's you know it there's  a lot of doing what a good philosopher  does which is to say so here's what the  source is saying here's what I like  about it here's what I find problematic  why do I find it problematic making all  of those critical distinctions that's  very important  um  I think you know the book successfully  makes its case uh so it it provides an  argument and it's a Well worked out  argument that you know  ought to motivate us to to want to do  things differently it's making the case  that Quest benefit analyzes are in fact  needed but they need to be improved and  the way that they're going to be  improved ultimately is by having  philosophers and not just any  philosophers because a lot of  philosophers would probably do a bad job  with this  bringing philosophers into the  discussion I think it makes a great case  for bringing applied philosophy into  greater communication with policy making  and with economics on that point  another thing that I do like about the  book that comes out at several different  points is when we're engaging in  thinking about people's preferences and  willingness to pay as you know something  that we can measure  carniadis mentions that institutions and  governments strictly speaking don't  actually have preferences as such  although they play a massive role not  just in making policy themselves right  but also in communicating with each  other about the policies that are going  to be made or advocated or or worked out  or you know whatever we're going to say  so that's that's a really critical point  uh to bring up and to think through  I think that another really  solid feature of the book is that you  know if you read this it's ranging over  a number of different  theories issues approaches assumptions  in economics in policy making in ethics  and moral philosophy  and doing a great job in not losing the  thread of the argument so anything that  appears to be just a digression actually  figures in here that's in here in a way  that's uh in some respects quite  admirable  you know  it's it's tough to write  extended arguments well and carnitis  clearly has some talent for that  um  it's also good on you know hitting on  the um problems with philosophers in  particular you know  why it is that so like he says  um as ideological purists philosophers  have an inclination to propose solutions  that may have negative practical  implications but are justified in theory  politicians on the other hand have a pro  proclivity for solutions that are  practically and politically viable even  when those Solutions are not justified  by the underlying theories so figuring  out how to connect these two together  that is really the the difficult stuff  right and if the politicians themselves  are not going to do it and the  likelihood is that they're not then  philosophers need to be advocating for  this or people who understand philosophy  need to be advocating for this in ways  that are very pragmatic another thing  that I think is particularly  interesting about this work and I would  say is a good point although in some  respects it can also work against the  book is the Myriad of as the authors  calling them thought experiments which  are mostly about you know this this  group over here prefers this to this  degree and they have this amount of uh  resources or income they're all fairly  quantitative  if we want to call them thought  experiments I would actually just call  them case studies or examples and I  think considered as case studies or  examples they do a lot of good work  in moving the general argument forward  and giving people ways of wrapping their  heads around what's what's going on I  mean you could you could chart them out  on paper if you wanted to and I think  that could be quite helpful so  you know the other thing that I'll say  too is I think that  um  carniades has done a really good job in  the writing of of this  and it's  I would say is clear and as  interesting as this kind of material  ought to be oh I'll point one other  thing out too there are a number of kind  of cool illustrations that you find  throughout the work and I myself like  those some people might not like that  sort of thing in what's essentially like  a policy argument book but I think these  are are pretty cool so that those are  the things that I really like about the  work  foreign  is there anything that's problematic  about the work I mean problematic is a  better word than bad because I don't  have any criticisms as such about the  work but I do think that there are some  things that might put certain readers  off I mean to begin with if you are the  kind of person who's like oh I just  don't understand math whatsoever I mean  I would say in that case you're probably  lying to yourself and you should like  start you know learning math what you  can do as an adult but there's some  people that are put off by quantitative  analysis and this book is full of  quantitative analysis in part because  that's that's the topic you're not going  to get away from that it is about cost  benefit uh you know approaches to things  which rely on quantification so there's  a lot of numbers in the work and some  readers might not get into that and they  I think there's a lot of readers who  might also find this a bit on the dry  side and that's unfortunate because I  kind of gather that as I said I think  the author made this as interesting as  the material can be made  while still writing for a a general  audience not just a bunch of policy  wonks or you know philosophers  but there's only so much you can do with  that so is that problematic maybe it  depends on the kind of reader there's  nothing actually wrong with the book  it's just it's not going to peel  necessarily to everybody  I also think you know calling the  examples thought experiments is a little  bit  ambitious you know it's it's like  um calling and you know 18 to 23 foot  boat a ship when it's not really such  but that's kind of a quibble on my part  so I don't I don't think that's really a  big deal or something that should keep  somebody from reading the work and uh  frankly that that's all that I have by  way of mild criticism  for the book  um  I on the whole I think it's quite good  so final thoughts about this work I  think having read through it that this  is actually a really good book and  I wish  that we had a population and readership  here in the United States that would  like eat this sort of stuff up because  it's I think it's making a really  important case and it's uh it's quite  needed and probably not just of course  in the U.S but throughout the the world  um  so  I recommend it I then again you know if  you really really don't like  quantitative analysis you might find it  a bit off-putting and dry but I think  that you know from an ethical  perspective this is actually making a  really significant contribution and I'd  also say and I'm going to say this by  way of closing  this is doing public philosophy really  well in in a particular mode of doing  public philosophy when it comes to the  intersection between policy makers  including politicians and you know  important institutions and economists  and philosophers this is really setting  down a bunch of important challenges  right at the heart of that so I I think  it's a quite a good book
 

